User shader quads: the in-scene shader primitive on a perspective face.

`paint_user_shader` (b0) draws a program over a screen-aligned box. This
adds `Window::paint_user_shader_quad`, the same primitive over four
corners. Covers blade (Linux, and macOS under `macos-blade`) and DirectX 11.

`UserShader` gains the homography split the way `PolychromeSprite` carries
it (b6): the affine rows and the perspective row. The template's vertex
stage projects through them and emits clip w, so the hardware divide
keystones the quad and `fs_user(uv)` still gets a perspective-correct 0..1
across the face. A flat `paint_user_shader` carries the unit transform and
(0, 0, 1, 0), where the new vertex math is the old one exactly. Clip
distances move to linear interpolation, since the mask is a screen rect.
The struct grows from 160 to 208 bytes; the WGSL spells the transform out
as plain fields, so the template adds no type name a user source could
collide with. The template's span test moves to 208, and a new test pins
the transform's offsets against naga's layout of the template, because
blade's own size check only fires on a GPU.

Only a program that can run as the in-scene primitive paints on a quad,
one `screen_pass_only` is false for. Any other logs once per program and
paints nothing. `resolution` and `mouse` stay the bounding box's: the
pointer is read in the box, untransformed, not on the face.

blade takes the template as WGSL. DirectX translates it at registration
(shader_translate.rs), so the vertex change rides that path with no
DirectX code of its own: the instances are a `ByteAddressBuffer` read at
naga's WGSL offsets and sized from the host struct. The Windows-only
`the_in_scene_primitive_translates_and_compiles` test is what checks that
FXC accepts the result. Metal draws no user shaders and is untouched.

Not a backport. A gpui bump carries this forward with b0, b6 and e3.

